SM(241,{"n":"Genesis 15.7-21","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Genesis 15.7-21.mp3","d":2501.5,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[25.04,"Verse 7. Then he said to him, I am the God who brought you out of Ur of the Chaldeans to give you this land and inherit it."],[36.16,"So why does he say that?"],[40.72,"Um so, well, what one other note, too."],[45.2,"So there is in uh some Madrashic Jewish interpretation."],[53.52,"Um of the questions that some of that interpretation tries to answer is why Abram?"],[61.33,"Like out of all the humans, I mean there weren't that many in 2000 BC humans compared to today, but there were still a lot of humans."],[67.49,"There were millions of humans, on the earth."],[70.07,"Um why this guy, in particular?"],[75.08,"Why does he get called?"],[76.36,"Why does he get the promises?"],[78.46,"Ultimately, that's a question we can't answer."],[81.6,"God did what he did."],[82.48,"It's like asking why the Theotokos."],[87.41,"Um But in this Madrashic Jewish interpretation, one of the things they lit on, is that uh he says it brought you out of Ur of the Chaldeans."],[99.2,"Well, Ur, in uh in addition to being the Sumerian word for city, which is where the name actually comes from, but that word in Hebrew can mean fire."],[112.84,"So I said, well, what if this means I brought you out of the fire of the Chaldeans?"],[117.32,"And so you get these big narratives."],[120.68,"Some of them put Abram at the building of the Tower of Babel, and like he refuses to worship idols, they throw him into a furnace."],[131.08,"Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego style, in Babylon."],[136.22,"And God delivers him out of it."],[138.16,"Out of the fire of the Chaldeans."],[140.18,"And so that then is hypothesizing that, oh, well, it's because he was. not worshiping pagan gods and this"],[147.5,"This is why God, this is why God chose him."],[151.63,"I present that as a curiosity."],[155.71,"The real answer is that, like, why the Theotokos?"],[159.07,"Why Saint Peter?"],[160.43,"Why Saint Andrew?"],[162.27,"Why Daniel? Why Isaiah?"],[165.76,"Why Saint Paul?"],[168.16,"This isn't something available to us."],[173.12,"Why God picks who he picks to do what he chooses them to do."],[178.0,"But I thought I would note it."],[179.6,"Also, uh, Ur of the Chaldeans uh is uh completely anachronistic."],[187.92,"The word did not exist in 2000 BC"],[188.28,"There are no Chaldeans, yeah."],[189.8,"There are no Chaldeans yet."],[194.12,"Chaldeans refers to specifically the Neo-Babylonian Empire."],[200.12,"So it would be like saying Native American tribe location of the Americans."],[206.12,"Yes. Calling them Native Americans in the 13th century."],[210.6,"Yeah. Yeah. Or saying this tribe lived near Lafayette, Louisiana."],[218.52,"Like in the thirteenth century."],[220.97,"Um yeah. So uh the Babyl Neo-Babylonian Empire is where Ur later was."],[231.63,"But much l.ater, like the exile"],[236.71,"So this is another place, at least, where the text has been updated since the time of Moses."],[245.05,"Because that's not a word that Moses ever encountered or knew."],[249.93,"Um so that was a later identifier."],[255.61,"It was probably originally."],[258.01,"I'm doing scare quotes because original doesn't exist and doesn't matter, but likely originally it just said from Ur."],[266.73,"Who brought you from Ur, to this land."],[269.15,"Eventually people didn't know where Ur was."],[271.11,"Yeah. And so they're clarifying for a later audience where that was in the world."],[275.92,"But overall, this is an identifier"],[278.04,"Uh to give you this land and inherit it."],[282.84,"Does the phrasing of that ring a bell with anybody?"],[287.4,"I am Yahweh your God who brought you out of U of the Chaldeans to give you this land."],[293.89,"Beginning of Exodus 20, beginning of the Ten Commandments."],[298.15,"I am Yahweh your God who brought you out of the land of Egypt."],[302.97,"Out of the house of bondage."],[304.81,"Um so this phrasing is very deliberate in terms of what's about to happen in the story."],[314.17,"So this is the language you use when you're initiating a covenant."],[322.61,"The word for that's usually translated covenant in Hebrew is berit."],[330.13,"The Greek word is deathiki."],[333.81,"That is the same word that's translated by King James translators as Testament."],[339.59,"So literally the Old Testament and the New Testament, that's the old covenant and the new covenant."],[346.95,"That's what the name means."],[349.83,"But so a bereit, um when people are explaining what a covenant is, uh we tend to use sort of modern examples that don't really work."],[362.88,"So sometimes people say a covenant is like a contract."],[366.2,"Well, testament you get like last will and testament."],[369.72,"But uh we tend to like a contract that two people sign, or a treaty, like between two countries."],[378.52,"Treaty is a little closer, but it refers to a particular type of treaty that was common in the ancient world uh at the time of Moses, and for a couple hundred years before and after, and that followed a particular format."],[395.96,"And uh scholars and other nerds refer to this as a suzerain vassal treaty or a Hittite suzerainty treaty or that kind of thing."],[406.44,"The reason the Hittites are involved is we have a bunch of examples on Hittite tablets of these that follow the same format as the ones in the Bible."],[417.52,"But it wasn't just the Hittites."],[419.44,"This was a common thing in the ancient Near East."],[422.4,"It's called a suzerain vassal treaty because it's issued by a suzerain by a great king or a high king."],[428.9,"We talked about this before when we were talking about Keterleomer."],[431.8,"You would have the king of a large city who would be able to sort of project his will through force of arms, through economic power, etc. over the neighboring cities and the neighboring kings."],[445.48,"Sometimes that just happened through the realities of economics and that kind of thing."],[450.52,"Sometimes that happened through war, you'd go and and conquer those cities make their kings your vassals, make them pay tribute."],[458.53,"But when that happened, so the king of Ur, we're in the Ur III period."],[464.55,"The king of Ur, you know, basically takes over by extortion or whatever other means another city."],[471.83,"He makes the king of that city his vassal."],[474.47,"And he issues one of these covenants to that vassal."],[481.43,"And that has a few parts, but the first part, the very first thing you do, is the great king, the high king, identifies himself in exactly this kind of language."],[496.39,"I am uh Shalubiuba of the Hittites."],[496.83,"I am whoever."],[506.92,"Um, and you know, here is what I have done."],[511.82,"I am the ruler of this city, I have conquered the lands of so and so, da-da-da-da-da-da, to identify himself."],[521.2,"And then after that, it outlines here's what uh you are required to do."],[530.61,"And then here is what I will do in return if you do what you're supposed to do."],[535.22,"So that would be like here's the tribute you offer me at this time."],[538.5,"Here's this, here's that, here's the rules"],[542.62,"If I go to war, you need to send me so many soldiers, etc."],[546.38,"etc. And then in return, usually primarily, I will protect you from foreign invasion, I will secure trade routes."],[554.96,"I will do these things."],[557.28,"And then it was uh here are the consequences if you break the treaty, which is usually I will come back and you will all die."],[568.62,"And that's sort of what we saw go on with Keterleomer and the king of Sodom."],[572.46,"Remember, he had made them vassals."],[574.1,"They decided not to be vassals anymore."],[576.54,"He came down there and made them vassals again."],[579.0,"Um, this is by using this language, this is telegraphing to us here in Genesis that God is about to issue a covenant to Abe to Abram."],[594.02,"He is making Abram his his vassal."],[597.3,"Now, the word that's used in Hebrew regarding a covenant isn't you issue a covenant or you write up a covenant, it's you cut a covenant."],[608.5,"Katif. You cut a covenant with someone, and we're about to see why."],[614.43,"So verse 8."],[618.55,"And he said, Master and Lord, how will I know I will inherit it?"],[623.47,"So he said to him, Bring me a three-year-old heifer, a three-year-old female goat, a three-year-old ram, a turtle dove, and a young pigeon."],[635.27,"This kind of sounds like Quests and some MMOs I've played."],[638.71,"Bring me this list of random animals."],[646.15,"Verse 10. So he brought all these to him and cut them in half down the middle, and placed each piece opposite the other, but he did not cut the birds in two."],[657.91,"And when the vultures came down on the carcasses, Abram drove them away."],[664.03,"So you notice he doesn't tell Abram to cut them in two."],[672.19,"So Abram knows what's going on here when he cuts them."],[676.51,"This is where the cutting of covenant comes from."],[679.23,"He goes and he gets the animals and he cuts them lengthwise in half."],[684.19,"He sort of butterflies them."],[686.35,"Which is hard to do with a heifer, but he doesn't."],[690.99,"So they get butterfly on the two sides."],[693.63,"And of course, while he's doing it with all these animals, this takes time."],[696.99,"So vultures and buzzards are trying to come and get it at the."],[700.43,"Obviously, they have aliens help them."],[702.43,"Yeah. And he's got cattle mutilations."],[705.07,"Is that weird?"],[706.75,"So he has to beat off the vultures and the buzzards and the right."],[711.79,"Drive them away."],[714.59,"This gets turned into an interesting thing."],[715.07,"uh There's a text, Second Temple Jewish text called The Apocalypse of Abraham, where that interprets uh these vultures and buzzards as being demons."],[740.31,"Because they know whatever it is, it's going to be bad things for them in the future."],[743.69,"Even though they're not sure exactly what's going on."],[749.13,"So all these things get split, get cut"],[751.69,"Are all those uh sacrificial animals?"],[754.25,"Yes. Yes, those are all clean sacrificial animals."],[758.49,"I'm reading about Noah"],[762.21,"Um, is are the sacrifices or have they been continuous or just sporadic within those generations?"],[772.93,"Yeah. No, they've been continuous, but they've been at just built constructed altars."],[777.41,"Remember, we talked about the that Abram sort of as he goes has been building these altars in these different places, sort of as he travels."],[786.85,"So there's not sort of one sanctuary where everybody goes to worship."],[791.57,"Yahweh of this uh Yes."],[794.29,"Yeah, yeah. And so they're building altars with stones."],[800.21,"Um we'll talk more about altar construction when we get in further into the Torah."],[806.49,"But one of the reasons they're they're actually not allowed when they build an altar to cut the stone."],[814.73,"They're supposed to use just the natural stone the way they find it, not like apply their own."],[821.29,"Well, this is pre mosaic law."],[825.13,"So shape naturally, or well, the so Genesis, as we've seen, sort of retrojects things from later in the Torah."],[837.63,"So we had the Sabbath all the way back in creation."],[840.35,"We have Noah bringing clean and unclean animals onto the Ark, and you're like."],[846.48,"Um, etc. So these things. get sort of projected right back"],[853.52,"And so the idea is that these things are actually built into the creation."],[861.6,"So the Sabbath is like built into the creation, into how God created things."],[866.9,"In Genesis 1."],[868.5,"It's not that these animals, God has decided that this animal is unclean and this animal is clean."],[874.82,"That's actually something in the nature of the animals."],[879.95,"At this point."],[881.31,"So it's because, and this is not the rabbinic Jewish understanding of this."],[888.11,"The rabbinic Jewish understanding of this is that Abram, Noah, these people sort of mystically knew the Torah already in advance."],[902.23,"But the traditional Christian interpretation of this is that no, these things are it's a realist interpretation, that these things are actually true."],[913.77,"That it's not just God making rules arbitrarily, but that this is based in reality."],[927.29,"Okay, so verse 12."],[928.77,"Now around sunset, a trance fell upon Abram, and behold, horror and great darkness fell upon him."],[940.85,"So what is this casting us back to this idea of him falling into a trance?"],[947.81,"Adams. When Eve is created."],[950.3,"When Eve was created."],[951.6,"Except that was a more positive thing."],[953.92,"Even though it was getting ripped in half."],[955.92,"That was still a more positive thing."],[959.09,"But this um is uh great darkness and horror."],[969.81,"Okay. Now, where in the future does this come back?"],[978.37,"Where is there darkness later in the Torah?"],[981.69,"On top of Mount Sinai."],[986.66,"On top of Mount Sinai."],[988.74,"We tend to, because of the New Testament, think of God as light."],[994.92,"Because we used to think of light good, darkness evil."],[998.54,"Therefore, God is light."],[1002.91,"But God is also hidden in darkness."],[1008.69,"Because this is going to become an important principle in the Torah that, and we already saw it in the expulsion from paradise."],[1017.26,"That sinful humanity, sinful anything, can't be in the presence of God's holiness."],[1026.5,"And people can't see God and live, even though he just saw the word of God and lived."],[1031.67,"God is shrouded in darkness."],[1033.85,"So that he can't be seen."],[1036.17,"He can't be known."],[1037.37,"He can't be comprehended with our mind."],[1041.98,"And when we see people come into the presence of God, the presence of the holiness of God in the Torah and throughout the Hebrew Bible."],[1053.92,"They aren't like, oh yay, cool."],[1056.56,"Or oh wow, awesome."],[1059.05,"Like, what does I day Isaiah say?"],[1061.93,"It's kind of bodlerized in the English as, woe is me, I am undone."],[1066.63,"Like an accurate translation would involve cursing, so I don't blame them for not putting it in the Bible, but he is hosed."],[1073.05,"Like he's like, I am wicked, I am sinful."],[1076.83,"It has suddenly become very apparent to me, and I should not be here right in the presence of holy God."],[1082.03,"This will go poorly."],[1083.45,"Um that's the kind of terror that this is talking about."],[1087.46,"That's the darkness of the terror this is talking about."],[1090.02,"It's not that he's in some, he's not in kind of dangers in like he's being attacked by something evil."],[1096.42,"It's that this is, even though again, see again, we see it here, there's this God who keeps appearing to him and he keeps having these conversations with."],[1107.96,"And then there's coming into the presence of God who's shrouded in darkness, whom he can't see, and which fills him with terror and dread."],[1118.09,"Because of his own sinfulness and uncleanness."],[1121.95,"So this is the latter encounter with God."],[1128.67,"But God speaks to him again, verse 13, then he said to Abram, Know for certain that your seed will be strangers in a land not their own, and will serve them, and they will afflict and humble them four hundred years."],[1144.19,"Of the Exodus"],[1145.43,"So what is this a prediction of?"],[1152.79,"The sojourn in Egypt."],[1155.59,"Yeah. Sojourn in Egypt."],[1160.25,"They're going to end up in a land not their own, not here."],[1165.41,"And they're gonna have to be humbled."],[1168.47,"For 400 years."],[1174.71,"Also, the nation they serve I will judge, and afterward they shall come out with great possessions."],[1182.31,"So he's saying this is gonna happen in the future."],[1184.23,"So this is a prediction of the Exodus."],[1186.61,"They're gonna become slaves in Egypt."],[1188.65,"They're gonna be humbled there, and then God's going to judge Egypt and deliver them."],[1198.17,"Verse 15, but as for you, you shall go to your fathers in peace, buried in a good old age."],[1205.93,"Then in the fourth generation they shall return here, for the sin of the Amorites is not yet filled up."],[1215.54,"So people are going to get excited because I'm going to very briefly talk about giants."],[1221.86,"Which the Amorites comes Amorites, the way we transliterate it, it comes from the Akkadian word Amuru, which literally means westerners, because it referred originally to a group of people who came from uh Western Syria, Lebanon, Palestine, that area, which is to the west of Akkad and Sumer."],[1259.53,"To the west of Mesopotamia."],[1261.77,"Uh one of the they invaded in the middle of the third millennium BC."],[1270.33,"Isn't Hammurabi one of them?"],[1272.01,"And Hammurabi is the most well-known one of them."],[1276.7,"And the Amuru, a big part of their claim to sort of dominion."],[1285.35,"Was that they claimed that they still had the secrets that were revealed by the gods before the flood to humanity."],[1295.59,"That no one else had."],[1297.83,"So is that ringing any bells from back when we were in Genesis 6 a few chapters ago with the Nephilim?"],[1305.73,"So they were claiming to have this divine wisdom and were practicing the same kind of rituals."],[1314.31,"Which is what identifies somebody as a giant clan."],[1316.79,"So what God is saying is, because he's identified him as what?"],[1321.88,"The person who brought you out of Ur to give you this land."],[1326.08,"But Abram is not going to receive that land during his lifetime."],[1334.85,"And so God is here telling him why."],[1338.93,"He tells him two reasons."],[1343.65,"The last reason is the one we just read."],[1346.85,"The sin of the Amorites is not yet filled up."],[1352.85,"And this is all the way through the Torah in Joshua, later on in the historical books of the Hebrew Bible, the Old Testament, this imagery of a cup being filled up, is used to describe the sins of particular people groups."],[1372.35,"So there is a certain tension, not within God, but within the way God works in the world."],[1379.63,"Because on one hand, God is just, and establishes justice and judges rightly."],[1386.55,"Well, put things back in order."],[1390.31,"And so that means if someone is wicked, it's going to look an awful lot like punishment for them when God comes and straightens things out."],[1399.98,"On the other hand, as we're told explicitly in Isaiah, God does not desire the death of the wicked man, but that he turn and live."],[1409.98,"God does not want to punish, he does not want to destroy."],[1410.94,"He wants nations to repent"],[1412.82,"He wants people to repent."],[1418.66,"That's what he wants."],[1420.5,"And so the tension there between those two is what we call mercy."],[1428.95,"That period of time when God allows our sin and wickedness to go unpunished."],[1438.2,"Allows things, tolerates things not being right for the sake of our potential repentance."],[1448.12,"And what is our repentance?"],[1449.56,"Our repentance is us making them right."],[1455.1,"It's us making things right."],[1456.62,"Not saying I'm sorry, God."],[1458.14,"It's us making things right."],[1459.74,"So God doesn't have to."],[1462.7,"This is what it means, the New Testament."],[1464.22,"If we judge ourselves, we will not be judged."],[1467.9,"If we put things right ourselves, God won't have to do it on the day of judgment."],[1474.62,"So there's this period of time."],[1478.54,"And what God is saying to Abram here is that for the Amorites, we're still in that period of time."],[1485.1,"There's still mercy."],[1486.38,"Now God knows they're not going to repent."],[1489.5,"That's why he says it's not full yet."],[1492.31,"He knows there's going to come a point where it's full."],[1495.59,"And when it's full, what that means is. on one hand, if we imagine justice like scales, we've got two scales"],[1503.29,"On one of these scales is his desire for the repentance of the wicked."],[1508.73,"On the other side of the scales is the cries of the innocent victim, like Abel's blood crying out of the ground in Genesis 4, a few chapters before this."],[1517.85,"Those two things are on the scale."],[1520.09,"And eventually, the cries of the victims."],[1524.71,"The cries of the oppressed."],[1526.57,"The people who have been harmed, who have been the victims of the evil, eventually it tilts."],[1533.53,"And when that happens, God comes in judgment."],[1537.85,"We're going to see this happen to Sodom in not too many chapters."],[1541.67,"Where it tilts, and that's it."],[1544.0,"So the time is going to come for the Amorites in the book of Joshua when that's going to be it."],[1552.02,"The time of repent for repentance is over, and now is the time for God to make things right."],[1558.1,"But he's saying that's not yet."],[1559.78,"And so I'm not going to take their land away from them and give it to you yet."],[1565.62,"Until this is done."],[1578.38,"They're going to spend this time in Egypt."],[1582.91,"And here it talks about humbling them."],[1584.59,"Egypt is going to be referred to later in the book of Exodus, the time in Egypt as a furnace"],[1591.35,"And we tend to think, oh, this is just horrible."],[1594.23,"They're going through this horrible trauma and this horrible experience."],[1597.51,"But a furnace is also what you use to refine things."],[1601.61,"To refine metal, to burn away the garbage and just leave the gold, the silver."],[1608.83,"The precious gems."],[1612.35,"And so this is going to be an experience of suffering, but suffering that will potentially has the potential to sanctify them."],[1626.55,"So God is here giving Abram more detail about what's going to happen in the next generations."],[1632.61,"And He's saying, I am going to this the immediate level."],[1639.83,"Because we've now seen there's sort of two layers to these promises."],[1644.09,"There's this stars layer, there's this salvation layer."],[1648.71,"That's the ultimate layer."],[1649.83,"And then there's this sort of physical layer of yes, you, Abram, are going to father a son."],[1656.2,"In history, in your lifetime."],[1658.76,"And a bunch of people are going to be descended from you, and they're going to come and they're going to live in this piece of land."],[1665.38,"So there's that layer."],[1667.06,"And those two layers, if you think about prophecy, the way prophecy works in the Old Testament, there's a sign that's given that's sort of immediate."],[1679.5,"And then that sign that's immediate gives you the proof, that the bigger piece that's off in the future is also going to happen."],[1691.44,"So a quick example of that."],[1694.96,"Luke chapter 2."],[1697.6,"Angels come to shepherds."],[1701.33,"Tell them the Messiah's been born."],[1705.51,"He's going to redeem Israel."],[1708.39,"And then what does the angel say?"],[1711.67,"This will be a sign for you."],[1714.59,"You will go and find a baby wrapped in swaddling clothes, lying in a manger."],[1720.24,"Go over here, you'll see a baby laying in a food trough."],[1723.28,"In an animal food trough in a cave with animals."],[1726.74,"That's not something you see every day."],[1728.8,"So if you go see that, that'll be a sign to you that we're telling you the truth and that that baby is the Messiah."],[1737.93,"This is also what when Jesus was preaching."],[1740.43,"The people come to him and ask for a sign."],[1743.07,"Oh, show us some sign so that we know what you're telling us is true."],[1746.76,"And Christ says, you know, woe to you, you know, cursed is the generation that that demands signs, or they won't believe."],[1755.25,"And so God is saying to Abram, look, you're not going to, I mean, you're gonna have this son."],[1761.99,"But you're not gonna see me keep this land promise during your lifetime."],[1769.27,"But I am, and here's why you're not gonna see it."],[1773.48,"And so you could you could still know, that this bigger promise in the future is true."],[1780.22,"This we saw this in Hebrews, the way Hebrews treats these promises."],[1784.3,"That the the physical promised land, them coming and living in that land for a couple centuries."],[1789.7,"That that was the sign."],[1791.14,"That wasn't the end itself, that wasn't the goal, that wasn't the purpose, that wasn't the promise."],[1796.26,"That was the sign. of the bigger promise of salvation of Theosis, that it was true"],[1802.22,"When you see X happen, you'll know that Y will happen."],[1806.78,"And correspondingly, because it happened, the land now becomes irrelevant."],[1813.42,"But that was a sign."],[1818.94,"It wasn't like, oh, we need to go back and find that manger and put the Eucharist in it or something."],[1824.72,"Like, that would be the same kind of thing."],[1827.14,"You're mistaking the sign for the prophecy."],[1833.04,"So so this is what God is getting at."],[1837.08,"You're not going to see this immediate sign."],[1838.92,"Here's why. Do you have a"],[1841.06,"But the rest of this will be true."],[1842.82,"And we're going to see then a further confirmation."],[1846.66,"I was just going to ask: are the emirants the ones who depicted their gods half-fish being something else?"],[1853.86,"Um you're thinking of the Apkalu, I think, the seven sages."],[1859.22,"Uh and that was in uh well."],[1868.02,"Yeah, because the old Babylonian dynasty had versions of those stories."],[1872.5,"So I was just, it seemed."],[1874.42,"Yes, but also other people."],[1876.18,"Yeah. It seems like that implies something about the flood."],[1879.38,"Yeah. Yeah, yeah."],[1881.7,"So the first of the seven sages is like part fish, part human, and comes up out of the out of the river, out of the underworld."],[1890.07,"Out of the deep and shares wisdom."],[1893.75,"Yeah. That's yeah."],[1897.99,"So when you were talking about the Pharisees asking Jesus for a sign, and he didn't give them a sign, there's one time at which he said you will not receive any sign, but the sign of the sign of Jonah."],[1909.35,"And that's kind of reversing it."],[1910.65,"The sign that you will receive is the sign that was already given in the past."],[1914.25,"Well which points to him."],[1916.25,"Is that kind of well, he was going to repeat it."],[1919.23,"Because this is this is one of the things a lot of people don't realize, but we'll emphasize when we get to Jonah."],[1923.55,"The reason we read Jonah on Holy Saturday, pretty much the whole book is one of the Old Testament readings of the baptismal liturgy."],[1930.87,"Uh, is that a lot of people don't realize this?"],[1933.99,"Jonah died. He wasn't sitting in monstro like with a campfire, like like in Pinocchio."],[1944.22,"The fish was Leviathan."],[1945.82,"Leviathan, he swims down into the abyss."],[1949.15,"Yeah. Yeah. And one of the we just had a couple weeks ago on Sunday, I noted it in one of the hymns, you know, it's from Jonah's point of view, and he says, you know, the the fish was my grave."],[1963.37,"Like that's and so the idea was he was three days at the fish, he was dead, and then God brought him back to life."],[1971.61,"So that's a way of Christ referring to because what was the sign to the Pharisees?"],[1978.45,"That Christ died and was in the tomb and he rose on the third day."],[1983.11,"That's the sign you'll receive."],[1986.37,"Will be the sign of Jonah."],[1987.73,"And what happened to Jonah was a sort of a type then of that Christ fulfilled."],[1993.11,"A pattern that Christ fulfilled."],[1994.79,"And which should have the Pharisees, when Christ rose from the dead, been like, oh, well, yes, everything he was saying was true because he rose from the dead."],[2002.8,"And God thereby vindicated him."],[2004.4,"Which, if you read the early chapters of Acts, that's exactly how they preach about Christ to those same Pharisees."],[2010.56,"They talk about God having vindicated him as the Messiah by raising him from the dead."],[2017.64,"So verse 17, it came to pass when the sun went down, that there was a flame."],[2024.36,"And behold, there appeared a smoking oven and lamps of fire that passed between those divided pieces."],[2031.8,"Okay. So he has the split animals, this flame."],[2042.73,"Which think later, where does this kind of thing show up?"],[2046.41,"A moving flame."],[2047.63,"The pillar of fire in the Exodus."],[2050.77,"That we would hold to be a manifestation of the Holy Spirit."],[2055.73,"So we've got in the Old Testament."],[2057.57,"So this coming in this Trinitarian debut."],[2061.47,"We saw the Word initiate it."],[2064.27,"We saw the darkness."],[2067.83,"The presence of the Father, and we see the Holy Spirit now, this manifestation of God's presence, moves between the pieces."],[2079.03,"So when this ritual is normally done, and what people would have been expecting, the expected pattern for this would have been for Abram to walk between the pieces."],[2090.81,"Because he's the vassal."],[2093.1,"He's the lesser party."],[2095.74,"And walking between the pieces was a way of taking an oath, saying, if I violate this covenant, if I violate what is expected of me, then may I be like these animals."],[2115.03,"Split in two, dead."],[2117.09,"Ripped apart. So this gets inverted, though."],[2122.93,"God, who is the higher party, passes through and takes this oath."],[2133.53,"That if this covenant is broken, God will be right split in two."],[2141.13,"Now, immediately, this would say to Abram. well, God can't die"],[2147.61,"So now I know, even though I'm not going to see my descendants living in the land, I know that this is true."],[2156.81,"Even though I'm not going to see that sign, I have now seen God take this oath."],[2163.79,"So I know God will keep it."],[2167.79,"But let's think a little further ahead in history."],[2172.43,"What's going to happen with this covenant?"],[2174.67,"Are his descendants going to stay in the land?"],[2178.91,"In the long life."],[2180.21,"Because they're not going to keep this covenant."],[2183.14,"They're going to violate it in every way they possibly can."],[2187.4,"They're going to break this covenant."],[2190.6,"And what how does God then fix that situation?"],[2198.04,"He dies. He has to become man to do it."],[2210.11,"But he dies."],[2214.03,"And so now, covenant renewed."],[2221.12,"The new covenant that we get in the New Testament is really a renewed covenant."],[2228.09,"Established covenant by Christ."],[2232.17,"Because he takes care of the violation of the old covenant."],[2237.79,"And restores it."],[2239.63,"So say we get Christ's death and resurrection."],[2241.95,"We get the whole gospel and the Holy Trinity right here at Genesis 15."],[2247.95,"And giants to boot."],[2249.47,"I mean, what more could you want in a chapter?"],[2253.23,"Giants are about to come back, too."],[2260.27,"So verse 18: on the same day the Lord made a covenant with Abram, saying, I will give this land to your seed from the river of Egypt to the great river, the river Euphrates, the Kenites, the Keniteses, theit Cesana,an theites Cadmonites, the Hittites, the Perizzes, the Rephaim, the Amorit, the Uites, the Gergeshites, and the Jebusites."],[2284.55,"That list will become familiar as we move through here."],[2289.11,"Those are all the giant clans."],[2308.07,"I have to say this because of our dispensationalist friends."],[2313.19,"Notice here, I will give this land to your seed plural."],[2318.59,"And then he describes that land."],[2321.55,"He does not say those are going to be the borders of the nation of Israel."],[2325.95,"Sorry, dispensationalists. He says that's the land that's going to go to Abraham's descendants."],[2335.79,"That gets fulfilled by the end of Joshua."],[2341.95,"Why? We'll see when we get to Deuteronomy chapter 2."],[2347.15,"God's going to go through all of the other Abrahamites."],[2353.55,"So he's going to talk about the Edomites who are descended from Esau and say, I brought them to this land, which is part of this big piece of land he just described."],[2362.95,"He's going to say, I brought them here."],[2364.55,"I gave this to the Edomites."],[2366.31,"They drove out a couple of these giant clans from the land of Edom."],[2371.43,"And I gave it to them."],[2372.39,"And guess what, Israel?"],[2373.43,"You're not allowed to take a single foot of that land, because it doesn't belong to you."],[2377.91,"I gave it to them."],[2379.37,"He's going to say the same thing about the Ammonites who are descended from Lot, the Moabites who are descended from Lot, the uh the Ishmaelites who become the Midianites."],[2392.03,"Like Jethro, Moses' father-in-law."],[2394.59,"All of these people are descended from Abraham."],[2397.31,"And all of them get pieces of this big swath of land."],[2401.51,"And at the end of all that listing in uh Deuteronomy chapter. 2, he says, and now I'm bringing Israel to this piece of it"],[2411.25,"For them to have this script."],[2413.65,"But it's not presenting Israel in regard to the land, in regard to the promises of Abraham, as different from those other descendants of Abraham."],[2423.98,"Other than firstborn status."],[2427.36,"But the others are still sons of Abraham too."],[2430.88,"And receive promises too."],[2434.64,"This is going to be a theme we're going to see over and over again as we move through the Torah."],[2438.72,"Breaking this thing we've got in our head."],[2441.6,"I think we all know where it came from."],[2444.18,"That the Old Testament is just about ethnic Israel or people, blood blo descodend. descended from Abrahamed from Jacob"],[2453.24,"And that's all the Old Testament is about."],[2456.44,"That's all the Torah is about."],[2457.72,"It's not. Wait till the Greeks show up."],[2461.88,"There's a bunch of Greeks who are going to show up later."],[2465.0,"Um so, but this is this right of chapter break, time-wise."],[2470.12,"This is a good place to stop for this evening."],[2472.68,"So we'll pick up in uh chapter 16 next week, Lord willing."],[2476.52,"Thanks, everybody. Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2486.44,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2491.8,"That's whole counsel at ancientfa.ith.com"]],"o":[{"t":25.04,"lv":2,"h":"15:7 - Out of Ur of the Chaldeans","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">God: \"I am the God who <b>brought you out of Ur of the Chaldeans</b> to give you this land\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Why does he say that?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Midrashic Jewish interpretation</b> asks <b>why Abram?</b></li><li class=\"d1\">millions of humans in 2000 BC - \"why this guy, in particular?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">ultimately <b>unanswerable</b> - \"God did what he did\" - like asking <b>why the Theotokos</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Midrash latches onto \"Ur\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Sumerian word for <b>city</b>; in Hebrew can mean <b>fire</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"brought you out of the <b>fire of the Chaldeans</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">big narratives: Abram at <b>Tower of Babel</b>, refuses idols, thrown in a <b>furnace</b> - Shadrach, Meshach, Abednego style - God delivers him</li><li class=\"d1\">so that's why God chose him</li><li class=\"d0\">He presents it \"as a <b>curiosity</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Real answer: why Theotokos? St. Peter? Andrew? Daniel? Isaiah? Paul?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>God picks who he picks</b> to do what he chooses them to do</li></ul>"},{"t":179.6,"lv":3,"h":"\"Chaldeans\" is anachronistic","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Chaldeans</b> did not exist in 2000 BC</li><li class=\"d1\">refers specifically to the <b>Neo-Babylonian Empire</b> - exile period, much later</li><li class=\"d1\">like calling a tribe <b>Native Americans</b> in the 13th c. or \"near <b>Lafayette, Louisiana</b>\" then</li><li class=\"d0\">Another place the text has been <b>updated since Moses</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not a word Moses ever knew</li><li class=\"d0\">Likely originally just \"<b>from Ur</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">people later didn't know where Ur was</li><li class=\"d1\">clarifying for a <b>later audience</b>; an identifier</li></ul>"},{"t":278.04,"lv":2,"h":"Covenant-initiating language","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"I am Yahweh your God who brought you out of...\" - ring a bell?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Exodus 20</b>, start of the <b>Ten Commandments</b>: \"brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Phrasing is <b>very deliberate</b> - the language for <b>initiating a covenant</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew <b><i>berit</i></b> = covenant; Greek <b><i>diatheke</i></b></li><li class=\"d1\">KJV translates as <b>Testament</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Old/New Testament = <b>old/new covenant</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Modern analogies don't work</li><li class=\"d1\">\"like a <b>contract</b>\" / last will and testament - no</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>treaty</b> closer, but a particular type</li></ul>"},{"t":395.96,"lv":2,"h":"Suzerain-vassal treaty","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Scholars and other nerds\": <b>suzerain-vassal treaty</b> / <b>Hittite suzerainty treaty</b></li><li class=\"d1\">common in ancient Near East ~Moses' time, couple hundred years either side</li><li class=\"d1\">Hittites: many examples on <b>tablets</b> matching the biblical format</li><li class=\"d0\">Issued by a <b>suzerain</b> - great king/high king</li><li class=\"d1\">king of a large city projects will by <b>arms</b> or <b>economic power</b> over neighboring kings</li><li class=\"d1\">conquer, make kings <b>vassals</b>, pay <b>tribute</b></li><li class=\"d1\">e.g. <b>king of Ur</b> (Ur III period)</li><li class=\"d0\">Format</li><li class=\"d1\">1. great king <b>identifies himself</b> - \"I am Shalubiuba of the Hittites... here is what I have done\"</li><li class=\"d1\">2. what <b>you must do</b> - tribute, soldiers if I go to war</li><li class=\"d1\">3. what <b>I will do</b> - protect from invasion, secure trade routes</li><li class=\"d1\">4. <b>consequences</b> if you break it - \"I will come back and you will all die\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Like <b>Chedorlaomer</b> and king of Sodom: made vassals, they rebelled, he came back</li><li class=\"d0\">So Genesis is <b>telegraphing</b>: God is making Abram his <b>vassal</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew: you don't \"issue\" a covenant, you <b>cut</b> a covenant - \"we're about to see why\"</li></ul>"},{"t":614.43,"lv":2,"h":"15:8-11 - Animals cut in half","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v8: \"Master and Lord, <b>how will I know</b> I will inherit it?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">God: bring <b>three-year-old heifer, goat, ram, turtledove, young pigeon</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"sounds like <b>quests</b> in MMOs\"</li><li class=\"d0\">v10: he cut them <b>in half down the middle</b>, each piece opposite the other; <b>birds not cut</b></li><li class=\"d0\">God <b>doesn't tell him to cut</b> - Abram <b>knows what's going on</b></li><li class=\"d1\">origin of <b>\"cutting\" a covenant</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"butterflies\" them lengthwise - hard with a heifer</li><li class=\"d0\">Vultures come down; Abram <b>drives them away</b></li><li class=\"d1\">takes time to do all the animals</li><li class=\"d1\">jokes: aliens, cattle mutilations</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Apocalypse of Abraham</b> (Second Temple Jewish text)</li><li class=\"d1\">vultures/buzzards = <b>demons</b></li><li class=\"d1\">they know it will be <b>bad for them</b> in the future, not sure how</li></ul>"},{"t":751.69,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: sacrifices and altars before Moses","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: those are all sacrificial animals? <b>Yes, clean</b> sacrificial animals</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: sacrifices continuous or sporadic since Noah?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>continuous</b>, on constructed <b>altars</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Abram building altars as he travels - <b>no one sanctuary</b></li><li class=\"d1\">altars of <b>uncut natural stone</b> - more in the Torah later</li><li class=\"d0\">Q (implied): this is <b>pre-Mosaic law</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Genesis <b>retrojects</b> things from later in the Torah</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Sabbath</b> back in creation; <b>clean/unclean</b> animals on Noah's ark</li><li class=\"d1\">built into <b>creation</b> - Sabbath is in how God created, Genesis 1</li><li class=\"d1\">clean/unclean = something <b>in the nature of the animals</b>, not arbitrary rules</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Rabbinic</b> view: Abram, Noah <b>mystically knew the Torah</b> in advance</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Traditional Christian</b> view: <b>realist</b> - these things are actually true, based in <b>reality</b></li></ul>"},{"t":927.29,"lv":2,"h":"15:12 - Trance, horror and great darkness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v12: sunset, a <b>trance</b> fell on Abram; \"<b>horror and great darkness</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Casts back to <b>Adam's trance</b> when <b>Eve</b> was created</li><li class=\"d1\">that was more positive, \"even though it was getting ripped in half\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Where does <b>darkness</b> come back? <b>Mount Sinai</b></li><li class=\"d0\">We think \"light good, darkness evil, therefore <b>God is light</b>\" (New Testament)</li><li class=\"d1\">but God is <b>also hidden in darkness</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sinful humanity can't be in the presence of God's holiness</b> - seen in expulsion from paradise</li><li class=\"d1\">people <b>can't see God and live</b>; shrouded so he can't be <b>seen, known, comprehended</b></li><li class=\"d0\">People in God's presence in the Hebrew Bible aren't \"oh wow, awesome\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Isaiah</b>: \"woe is me, I am undone\" - accurate translation would involve cursing - \"he is hosed\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I am wicked... I should not be here\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not being attacked by something evil - it's <b>terror before holy God</b> because of his own <b>sinfulness and uncleanness</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So this is the <b>latter encounter</b> with God (after the Word speaking)</li></ul>"},{"t":1128.67,"lv":2,"h":"15:13-16 - Four hundred years","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v13: \"your seed will be <b>strangers in a land not their own</b>... afflict and humble them <b>400 years</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Prediction of the <b>sojourn in Egypt</b> - slaves, humbled</li><li class=\"d0\">\"the nation they serve <b>I will judge</b>\" - they come out with <b>great possessions</b></li><li class=\"d1\">prediction of the <b>Exodus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">v15: Abram will die in peace, <b>good old age</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"in the <b>fourth generation</b> they shall return here\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"for the <b>sin of the Amorites is not yet filled up</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1215.54,"lv":2,"h":"The Amorites and the giants","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"People are going to get excited - I'm going to briefly talk about <b>giants</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Amorite</b> from Akkadian <b><i>Amuru</i></b> = <b>westerners</b></li><li class=\"d1\">from Western Syria, Lebanon, Palestine - west of Mesopotamia</li><li class=\"d1\">invaded mid-<b>3rd millennium BC</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Hammurabi</b> most well-known (Q from audience)</li><li class=\"d0\">Claim to dominion: they <b>still had the secrets revealed by the gods before the flood</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ring a bell? <b>Genesis 6, Nephilim</b></li><li class=\"d1\">claimed <b>divine wisdom</b>, practiced same rituals</li><li class=\"d1\">= what identifies a <b>giant clan</b></li><li class=\"d0\">God identified himself as the one who brought Abram out of Ur to give the land</li><li class=\"d0\">But Abram <b>won't receive it in his lifetime</b> - God tells him why</li></ul>"},{"t":1338.93,"lv":3,"h":"Cup filling, mercy and justice","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Two reasons</b> - last is the Amorites' sin not yet full</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Cup being filled</b> imagery: Torah, <b>Joshua</b>, historical books - sins of people groups</li><li class=\"d0\">Tension - \"not within God, but within the way God works in the world\"</li><li class=\"d1\">God is <b>just</b>; if someone is wicked, <b>correction looks like punishment</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but <b>Isaiah</b>: God does <b>not desire the death of the wicked</b>, but that he turn and live</li><li class=\"d0\">Between them = <b>mercy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">period God <b>tolerates</b> things not being right for our potential <b>repentance</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>repentance = us making things right</b>, \"not saying I'm sorry, God\"</li><li class=\"d1\">NT: \"if we <b>judge ourselves</b>, we will not be judged\" - so God doesn't have to</li><li class=\"d0\">For the Amorites, still the mercy period; God knows they <b>won't repent</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Two <b>scales</b></li><li class=\"d1\">his desire for <b>repentance</b> vs <b>cries of the innocent victim</b> (Abel's blood, Genesis 4)</li><li class=\"d1\">eventually it <b>tilts</b> → God comes in judgment</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Sodom</b> a few chapters on; Amorites in <b>Joshua</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So not yet: \"I'm not going to take their land and give it to you yet\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1578.38,"lv":2,"h":"Egypt as furnace; two layers of promise","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Time in Egypt - \"<b>humbling</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Exodus later calls Egypt a <b>furnace</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we think just horrible trauma</li><li class=\"d1\">but a furnace <b>refines</b>: burn away garbage, leave <b>gold, silver, gems</b></li><li class=\"d1\">suffering with potential to <b>sanctify</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Two layers to the promises</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>stars / salvation</b> layer = <b>ultimate</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>physical layer</b>: Abram fathers a son in history, descendants live in this land</li><li class=\"d0\">How <b>prophecy</b> works in the OT</li><li class=\"d1\">an <b>immediate sign</b> proves the bigger piece in the future will happen</li></ul>"},{"t":1691.44,"lv":3,"h":"Sign versus prophecy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Example: <b>Luke 2</b>, angels to shepherds</li><li class=\"d1\">Messiah born, will redeem Israel</li><li class=\"d1\">\"this will be a <b>sign</b>\": baby in <b>swaddling clothes in a manger</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"animal food trough in a cave with animals - not something you see every day\"</li><li class=\"d0\">People ask Jesus for a sign - \"<b>cursed is the generation that demands signs</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">God to Abram: you'll have the son, but <b>won't see the land promise</b> kept in your lifetime</li><li class=\"d1\">here's why - and you can still know the bigger promise is true</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Hebrews</b>: physical promised land for a couple of centuries = the <b>sign</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not the goal or the promise</li><li class=\"d1\">sign of the bigger promise: <b>salvation, theosis</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"when you see X, you'll know Y\" - then the <b>land becomes irrelevant</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Mistake: \"go find that <b>manger</b> and put the <b>Eucharist</b> in it\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>mistaking the sign for the prophecy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">More confirmation coming</li></ul>"},{"t":1846.66,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: Apkallu and the flood","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Amorites depicted gods half-fish?</li><li class=\"d1\">thinking of the <b>Apkallu</b>, the <b>seven sages</b></li><li class=\"d1\">old Babylonian dynasty had versions</li><li class=\"d0\">First sage part fish, part human, comes out of the <b>river, underworld, deep</b> and shares <b>wisdom</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: implies something about the <b>flood</b>? Yes</li></ul>"},{"t":1897.99,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: The sign of Jonah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Jesus: no sign but <b>sign of Jonah</b> - reversing it, sign already given in the past?</li><li class=\"d1\">he <b>repeats</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jonah</b> read on <b>Holy Saturday</b>, nearly whole book, one of the <b>baptismal liturgy</b> readings</li><li class=\"d0\">Jonah <b>died</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not sitting in \"<b>Monstro</b>\" with a campfire, Pinocchio</li><li class=\"d1\">fish was <b>Leviathan</b>, swims down into the <b>abyss</b></li><li class=\"d1\">hymn from Jonah's view: \"the fish was my <b>grave</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">three days, dead, then God brought him back</li><li class=\"d0\">Sign to the Pharisees = Christ <b>died, in tomb, rose third day</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jonah = <b>type</b>, pattern Christ fulfilled</li><li class=\"d1\">Pharisees should have said: everything he said was true - <b>vindicated</b> by resurrection</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>early Acts</b>: preach to those same Pharisees - God <b>vindicated him as Messiah</b> by raising him</li></ul>"},{"t":2017.64,"lv":2,"h":"15:17 - Smoking oven and lamps of fire","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v17: sun down, a <b>flame</b>; <b>smoking oven and lamps of fire</b> pass between the divided pieces</li><li class=\"d0\">Where else? A moving flame - <b>pillar of fire in the Exodus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= manifestation of the <b>Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Trinitarian debut</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Word</b> initiated it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>darkness</b> = presence of the <b>Father</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Holy Spirit</b> = God's presence moving between the pieces</li></ul>"},{"t":2079.03,"lv":3,"h":"God passes between the pieces","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Expected: <b>Abram</b> walks between - he's the <b>vassal</b>, lesser party</li><li class=\"d1\">walking between = taking an <b>oath</b>: if I violate this, \"may I be like these animals - <b>split in two, dead, ripped apart</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Inverted</b>: <b>God</b>, the higher party, passes through and takes the oath</li><li class=\"d1\">if the covenant is broken, <b>God will be split in two</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Immediately to Abram: \"God can't die\" → I know it's true</li><li class=\"d1\">won't see the sign, but has seen <b>God take this oath</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Think further ahead: will descendants stay in the land?</li><li class=\"d1\">they'll <b>violate it in every way possible</b></li><li class=\"d1\">how does God fix it? <b>He dies</b> - had to <b>become man</b> to do it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>covenant renewed</b> - New Testament = a <b>renewed covenant</b>, established by <b>Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he takes care of the violation of the old and restores it</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ's death and resurrection, the whole gospel and the <b>Holy Trinity</b> in <b>Genesis 15</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"and <b>giants</b> to boot\" - giants about to come back too</li></ul>"},{"t":2260.27,"lv":2,"h":"15:18-21 - Boundaries and the clans","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v18: same day, covenant: land \"from the <b>river of Egypt</b> to the great river, the <b>Euphrates</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">list: Kenites, Kenizzites, Kadmonites, Hittites, Perizzites, <b>Rephaim</b>, Amorites, Canaanites, Girgashites, Jebusites</li><li class=\"d1\">list becomes familiar - \"those are all the <b>giant clans</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2308.07,"lv":3,"h":"\"Your seed\" - and the other Abrahamites","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">For <b>dispensationalist</b> friends: \"I will give this land to your <b>seed</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">he describes the land; does <b>not</b> say those are the <b>borders of Israel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">fulfilled by <b>end of Joshua</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Deuteronomy 2</b>: God lists the other Abrahamites</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Edomites</b> (Esau) - drove out giant clans, \"I gave this to the Edomites\"; Israel <b>not allowed a single foot</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same for <b>Ammonites</b> and <b>Moabites</b> (Lot), <b>Ishmaelites</b> → <b>Midianites</b> (<b>Jethro</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">all descended from Abraham, <b>all get pieces</b> of the big swath</li><li class=\"d1\">then \"now I'm bringing Israel to <b>this piece</b> of it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Israel not different from other descendants <b>except firstborn status</b></li><li class=\"d1\">others are sons of Abraham, <b>receive promises too</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Theme over and over: breaking the idea OT is <b>only ethnic Israel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>Wait till the Greeks show up</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Chapter break - good place to stop</li></ul>"}],"next":"Next week: <b>Genesis 16</b>"});
